1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,353
Application number one hundred.

2
00:00:02,755 --> 00:00:04,258
"We regret to inform you."

3
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:07,568
You have read that sentence
one hundred times.

4
00:00:07,867 --> 00:00:11,992
Fourteen months from now, the company
that sent it will pay you to teach its

5
00:00:11,992 --> 00:00:12,589
recruiters.

6
00:00:12,894 --> 00:00:16,376
But tonight, you are stacking
shelves at two in the morning.

7
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:20,045
You are twenty-seven.

8
00:00:20,045 --> 00:00:24,611
You have a degree, a student loan,
and a name badge that says trainee.

9
00:00:24,983 --> 00:00:26,929
You did everything they told you to do.

10
00:00:27,220 --> 00:00:30,490
Study hard. Get the paper.
The job will follow.

11
00:00:31,087 --> 00:00:32,620
The job did not follow.

12
00:00:33,098 --> 00:00:37,836
So you work the night shift, ten at night
until six in the morning, putting tins on

13
00:00:37,836 --> 00:00:38,293
shelves.

14
00:00:38,704 --> 00:00:42,236
The shop is so quiet at four in the
morning that you can hear the fridges

15
00:00:42,236 --> 00:00:42,720
breathing.

16
00:00:43,265 --> 00:00:46,004
You sleep in the same bedroom
you had when you were twelve.

17
00:00:46,411 --> 00:00:50,777
Every morning, your mom leaves a plate in
the microwave, and a note that says, any

18
00:00:50,777 --> 00:00:51,043
news?

19
00:00:51,452 --> 00:00:53,144
There is never any news.

20
00:00:53,578 --> 00:00:55,995
The loan company sends
a letter every month.

21
00:00:55,995 --> 00:00:58,412
The number on it only
goes in one direction.

22
00:00:58,819 --> 00:01:00,943
You stopped opening the
envelopes in March.

23
00:01:01,308 --> 00:01:05,884
At family dinner, Uncle Ray has a
theory. "Nobody wants to work anymore."

24
00:01:06,213 --> 00:01:08,744
You have applied for ninety-nine jobs.

25
00:01:08,862 --> 00:01:12,841
Your friend Dex got hired in a week.
His dad plays golf with the owner.

26
00:01:13,128 --> 00:01:15,457
"Honestly, man. Just put
yourself out there."

27
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:18,051
You have been out there for two years.

28
00:01:18,199 --> 00:01:23,447
Two years of cover letters. Two years of
writing, I am passionate about logistics.

29
00:01:23,447 --> 00:01:25,943
You are not passionate about logistics.

30
00:01:26,194 --> 00:01:28,223
And the worst part is not the no.

31
00:01:28,496 --> 00:01:31,966
It is the silence. Most of
them never answer at all.

32
00:01:33,266 --> 00:01:36,281
Then, finally, somebody calls you back.

33
00:01:36,492 --> 00:01:39,317
You iron your only shirt. Twice.

34
00:01:39,570 --> 00:01:43,172
You practise your answers in the bathroom
mirror until three in the morning.

35
00:01:43,518 --> 00:01:47,253
You even look up the name of the
company's founder, in case it comes up.

36
00:01:47,253 --> 00:01:48,290
It does not come up.

37
00:01:48,679 --> 00:01:51,334
The hiring manager is called Mr. Graves.

38
00:01:51,534 --> 00:01:54,777
He looks at your résumé for
four seconds. You count.

39
00:01:55,072 --> 00:01:58,684
"Night shift. Supermarket.
And you want to work here?"

40
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:01,133
"You are just not what
we are looking for."

41
00:02:01,895 --> 00:02:04,525
You thank him. You actually thank him.

42
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:09,304
You walk out past a wall of framed awards,
and you feel about three inches tall.

43
00:02:09,659 --> 00:02:13,867
On the bus home, the email arrives
anyway. Number one hundred.

44
00:02:14,107 --> 00:02:19,929
That night, you open a spreadsheet. You
have logged every application. Every date.

45
00:02:19,929 --> 00:02:20,923
Every silence.

46
00:02:21,136 --> 00:02:23,892
One hundred rows. All of them red.

47
00:02:24,146 --> 00:02:28,797
And for the first time, you say the
thing out loud. "Maybe it is me."

48
00:02:30,397 --> 00:02:34,850
Two days later, you are back in the same
building. Delivering their lunch order.

49
00:02:35,086 --> 00:02:38,865
The lift doors close, and you are
standing next to a woman with a lanyard.

50
00:02:39,126 --> 00:02:41,867
She looks at you. "You
interviewed on Thursday."

51
00:02:42,129 --> 00:02:46,486
She is a recruiter. Her name
is Nora. She looks very tired.

52
00:02:46,601 --> 00:02:49,722
And because it is a long lift
ride, she tells you the truth.

53
00:02:49,976 --> 00:02:53,017
"Nobody read your résumé.
Not a person, anyway."

54
00:02:53,162 --> 00:02:55,108
"A piece of software reads it first.

55
00:02:55,108 --> 00:02:58,242
If it cannot find the right
words, we never even see you."

56
00:02:58,292 --> 00:03:03,173
"And when a human does look, you get a
few seconds. That is all anybody gets."

57
00:03:03,238 --> 00:03:06,432
"You are not being rejected.
You are not being seen."

58
00:03:07,532 --> 00:03:10,977
The doors open. She walks
out. You forget to get off.

59
00:03:11,168 --> 00:03:15,257
You stand there holding six sandwiches,
trying to work out how many of your

60
00:03:15,257 --> 00:03:16,620
hundred were ever opened.

61
00:03:16,726 --> 00:03:19,262
Two years. One hundred applications.

62
00:03:19,262 --> 00:03:23,277
And you were playing a game
nobody told you the rules of.

63
00:03:24,777 --> 00:03:28,718
That night, you do not sleep.
You open the spreadsheet again.

64
00:03:29,078 --> 00:03:32,569
But this time, you are not looking
at it like a list of failures.

65
00:03:32,793 --> 00:03:34,660
You are looking at it like data.

66
00:03:34,841 --> 00:03:39,502
One hundred job adverts. One
hundred versions of your résumé.

67
00:03:39,502 --> 00:03:41,030
One hundred results.

68
00:03:41,320 --> 00:03:43,674
Nobody else on earth has this spreadsheet.

69
00:03:43,944 --> 00:03:49,170
A hundred failures, perfectly organised.
It might be the most useful thing you own.

70
00:03:49,446 --> 00:03:52,591
So you start reading the adverts
the way the software reads them.

71
00:03:52,858 --> 00:03:57,167
The same words keep showing up. The
words they are actually searching for.

72
00:03:57,529 --> 00:04:00,399
Your résumé does not contain
a single one of them.

73
00:04:00,673 --> 00:04:05,383
You wrote, hard worker. They were
searching for, inventory management.

74
00:04:05,637 --> 00:04:09,194
You stocked forty thousand
items a week on a night shift.

75
00:04:09,194 --> 00:04:11,004
That is inventory management.

76
00:04:11,232 --> 00:04:14,713
You were never unqualified.
You were untranslated.

77
00:04:15,558 --> 00:04:20,830
So you rewrite everything. One page.
Their words. Your numbers at the top.

78
00:04:21,226 --> 00:04:22,660
It takes you nine nights.

79
00:04:22,660 --> 00:04:26,331
You do it on your breaks, sitting
on a crate of tinned tomatoes.

80
00:04:26,544 --> 00:04:28,741
And then you send out ten.

81
00:04:29,441 --> 00:04:32,742
You go back to stacking tins,
and you try not to hope.

82
00:04:33,285 --> 00:04:36,247
Day three. Your phone
buzzes in aisle nine.

83
00:04:36,444 --> 00:04:37,227
An interview.

84
00:04:37,468 --> 00:04:39,535
Then another. Then two more.

85
00:04:39,604 --> 00:04:44,547
Ten applications. Four interviews.
After one hundred, and zero.

86
00:04:45,392 --> 00:04:48,836
You drop a tin of beans on your
foot. You do not even feel it.

87
00:04:49,049 --> 00:04:53,388
Three weeks later, you have a job
offer. A real one. With a desk.

88
00:04:53,438 --> 00:04:56,735
Your mom cries. Uncle Ray
says it was about time.

89
00:04:56,917 --> 00:04:58,949
And that could have been
the end of the story.

90
00:04:59,192 --> 00:05:02,514
But on your last night shift,
Sam is crying in the stock room.

91
00:05:02,709 --> 00:05:07,634
He got laid off from his day job. He
has sent sixty applications. Nothing.

92
00:05:07,890 --> 00:05:10,969
You sit down on the crate next to
him, and you open your laptop.

93
00:05:11,142 --> 00:05:15,828
You show him the word lists. You rewrite
his first page with him, line by line.

94
00:05:16,051 --> 00:05:18,901
Two weeks later, Sam has three interviews.

95
00:05:18,951 --> 00:05:22,433
And that is when you understand
what you are actually holding.

96
00:05:23,133 --> 00:05:25,731
You turn it into something
anybody can use.

97
00:05:25,845 --> 00:05:29,757
The word lists. The one page
layout. The before and after.

98
00:05:29,757 --> 00:05:32,928
Everything you learned
from a hundred failures.

99
00:05:33,143 --> 00:05:36,157
You call it the Callback
Kit. Twelve dollars.

100
00:05:36,348 --> 00:05:38,801
You build a page. You press publish.

101
00:05:39,104 --> 00:05:39,697
Nothing.

102
00:05:40,154 --> 00:05:43,637
You refresh the page so many times
that your thumb starts to hurt.

103
00:05:43,979 --> 00:05:48,203
Day four. One comment. "Résumé
templates are free, bro."

104
00:05:48,379 --> 00:05:52,401
Day nine. Dex shares it in the
group chat, with a laughing face.

105
00:05:52,533 --> 00:05:56,520
Uncle Ray asks why you are selling
homework, when you just got a real job.

106
00:05:56,820 --> 00:06:00,249
Even your mom looks worried.
"Do not lose the desk, honey."

107
00:06:00,488 --> 00:06:02,709
Two weeks. Zero sales.

108
00:06:02,871 --> 00:06:05,624
And the voice comes back. Maybe it is me.

109
00:06:05,881 --> 00:06:08,812
But you have a spreadsheet
that says the voice is wrong.

110
00:06:08,962 --> 00:06:13,409
So you ask the question you asked before.
What are they actually searching for?

111
00:06:13,694 --> 00:06:15,787
Nobody is searching for a template.

112
00:06:15,787 --> 00:06:18,656
They are searching for an
answer to the silence.

113
00:06:18,906 --> 00:06:22,029
So you stop selling the
kit. And you show the wall.

114
00:06:22,232 --> 00:06:25,716
You post all one hundred
rejections. Every one of them.

115
00:06:25,950 --> 00:06:27,547
And underneath, one line.

116
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:31,316
"Here is what I changed before
number one hundred and one."

117
00:06:31,620 --> 00:06:33,843
You post it at midnight,
and you go to sleep.

118
00:06:34,207 --> 00:06:36,846
You wake up to forty-one thousand views.

119
00:06:37,532 --> 00:06:40,125
And one notification you
have never seen before.

120
00:06:40,430 --> 00:06:44,421
Twelve dollars. From a nurse, in
a city you have never been to.

121
00:06:45,394 --> 00:06:47,531
By lunchtime, there are thirty more.

122
00:06:47,727 --> 00:06:50,555
You answer every single
message. All of them.

123
00:06:50,555 --> 00:06:55,457
It takes until four in the morning, and
for once, you do not mind being awake.

124
00:06:55,507 --> 00:06:57,778
People send you their old résumés.

125
00:06:57,778 --> 00:07:00,986
You tear one apart on
camera, kindly, every day.

126
00:07:01,234 --> 00:07:06,201
Then comes the first angry message. "I
bought your kit. I still got rejected."

127
00:07:06,451 --> 00:07:10,999
You could delete it. Instead, you
reply in public. "Send it to me.

128
00:07:10,999 --> 00:07:13,204
I will rewrite it myself. Free."

129
00:07:13,488 --> 00:07:15,220
It takes you four hours.

130
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:20,446
Nine days later, she posts again. A photo
of an office badge. With her name on it.

131
00:07:20,665 --> 00:07:24,493
That post sells more kits than everything
you ever wrote about yourself.

132
00:07:24,711 --> 00:07:29,324
Because nobody trusts a stranger's success
story. They trust a stranger's badge.

133
00:07:29,506 --> 00:07:32,467
You add an interview guide.
Then a salary script.

134
00:07:32,467 --> 00:07:35,246
Each one built from a
question somebody asked.

135
00:07:35,337 --> 00:07:38,482
Twelve dollars at a time, it
stops looking like homework.

136
00:07:38,550 --> 00:07:41,499
And one morning, you hand
in a badge of your own.

137
00:07:42,799 --> 00:07:47,453
Fourteen months after application one
hundred, forty thousand people have used

138
00:07:47,453 --> 00:07:48,586
something you made.

139
00:07:48,880 --> 00:07:50,582
Nurses. Drivers. Graduates.

140
00:07:50,582 --> 00:07:55,497
People who were told for years that they
were not what anyone was looking for.

141
00:07:55,955 --> 00:07:59,814
The loan letter comes one
last time. It says zero.

142
00:08:00,255 --> 00:08:02,703
You move out of the bedroom
with the rocket posters.

143
00:08:02,849 --> 00:08:03,821
You hire Sam.

144
00:08:04,127 --> 00:08:06,109
And you leave a note in
your mom's microwave.

145
00:08:06,412 --> 00:08:09,513
It says, news. Check your mortgage.

146
00:08:11,213 --> 00:08:13,045
And then, they come back.

147
00:08:13,265 --> 00:08:15,112
Dex's company goes under.

148
00:08:15,112 --> 00:08:19,175
He asks, quietly, if the kit
works for people like him.

149
00:08:19,449 --> 00:08:23,273
You send it to him for free.
And you do not make a joke.

150
00:08:23,427 --> 00:08:27,982
Uncle Ray's son cannot find a job.
Suddenly, the theory changes.

151
00:08:28,096 --> 00:08:31,170
And then an email arrives
from a company you recognise.

152
00:08:31,274 --> 00:08:35,657
They are losing good candidates. They
want someone to train their recruiters.

153
00:08:35,657 --> 00:08:37,536
They have heard you are the best.

154
00:08:37,664 --> 00:08:42,775
You walk into the same glass office. Nora
is in the front row. She is smiling.

155
00:08:43,140 --> 00:08:46,543
Mr. Graves is in the second
row. He does not recognise you.

156
00:08:46,708 --> 00:08:51,870
You put one slide on the screen. A
rejection email. Dated a Thursday.

157
00:08:51,870 --> 00:08:52,917
Signed by him.

158
00:08:53,104 --> 00:08:56,117
You give him four seconds
to read it. You count.

159
00:08:57,779 --> 00:08:59,641
Nobody in the room breathes.

160
00:08:59,768 --> 00:09:04,536
Then you click to the next slide, and you
teach them how to actually see people.

161
00:09:04,686 --> 00:09:07,688
You were never one hundred
rejections away from a job.

162
00:09:08,042 --> 00:09:10,874
You were one conversation
away from the rules.

163
00:09:11,123 --> 00:09:15,733
So whatever keeps telling you no,
write it down. That is not a wall.

164
00:09:15,733 --> 00:09:17,224
That is your research.

165
00:09:18,082 --> 00:09:22,332
If you want to learn how to do it,
comment, learn. And subscribe.

166
00:09:22,332 --> 00:09:25,600
Because next week, somebody
else starts from zero.
